    "id": "u-equals-u",
    "title": "U=U: HIV Treatment, Viral Suppression and Sexual Relationships",
    "category": "Education",
    "art": "rose",
    "excerpt": "What does undetectable equals untransmittable mean in everyday decisions?",
    "body": "<p>U=U stands for undetectable equals untransmittable. CDC explains that a person with HIV who gets and maintains an undetectable viral load will not transmit HIV through sex. Understanding that fact can replace unnecessary fear with more accurate conversations about health, treatment and intimacy. <a class=\"citation-link\" href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/hiv/treatment/index.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Source 1: CDC\">[1]</a></p>\n<p>It is a statement about HIV and sexual transmission under the relevant treatment conditions. It is not a description of someone's value, honesty or eligibility for affection.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understand-the-words-before-the-assumptions\">Understand the words before the assumptions</h2>\n<p>Viral load is a measure of the amount of HIV in the blood. HIV treatment, also called antiretroviral therapy, can suppress that amount. “Undetectable” refers to laboratory detection, not the disappearance of HIV from the body or a cure. Maintaining suppression involves effective treatment and clinical monitoring. <a class=\"citation-link\" href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/hiv/treatment/index.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Source 1: CDC\">[1]</a></p>\n<p>Different laboratory reports and clinical discussions may use different terms or thresholds. Ask the treating clinician what a result means in the context of sustained viral suppression. Feeling well, taking a first dose or having an old result are not interchangeable with an established treatment-and-monitoring plan.</p>\n<h2 id=\"keep-the-scope-precise\">Keep the scope precise</h2>\n<p>U=U concerns sexual HIV transmission. Other STIs and pregnancy are separate questions. A couple may choose condoms for those reasons even when sexual HIV transmission is prevented through maintained viral suppression. PrEP may be discussed according to the HIV-negative person's preferences and circumstances, including uncertainty about suppression or exposures outside the relationship. <a class=\"citation-link\" href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/hiv/treatment/index.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Source 1: CDC\">[1]</a> <a class=\"citation-link\" href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/hiv/prevention/prep.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Source 2: CDC\">[2]</a></p>\n<p>Do not extend the sexual-transmission statement into an identical claim about every other route of exposure. A clinician should guide questions involving pregnancy, breastfeeding or injection equipment. Precision protects the usefulness of U=U; it does not weaken the fact it describes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"talk-about-care-without-turning-a-partner-into-a-supervisor\">Talk about care without turning a partner into a supervisor</h2>\n<p>People can discuss treatment and monitoring respectfully without requiring constant proof, device access or a complete medical history. Start by identifying the decision you need to make together and the information relevant to it.</p>\n<p>An original example is: “I understand that maintained viral suppression prevents sexual HIV transmission. I would like to talk about what we each know, what questions we want answered and our separate STI and pregnancy-prevention plans.”</p>\n<p>The person receiving HIV care remains the person making their medical decisions. A partner can offer transport, appointment support or help with a practical problem if invited. Support should not become a system of inspection or punishment.</p>\n<h2 id=\"know-what-to-do-when-circumstances-change\">Know what to do when circumstances change</h2>\n<p>If treatment is interrupted, a dose or injection is missed, or a viral-load result is uncertain, ask the HIV care team what it means and what prevention is appropriate. Do not assume that one missed dose proves transmission risk, or that continued suppression can be guaranteed without assessment. <a class=\"citation-link\" href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/hiv/treatment/index.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Source 1: CDC\">[1]</a></p>\n<p>For a recent potentially significant exposure with uncertain suppression, urgent PEP assessment may be relevant. CDC recommends starting indicated PEP as soon as possible and within the applicable 72-hour window; a routine appointment later is not a substitute for that assessment. <a class=\"citation-link\" href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/hivnexus/hcp/pep/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Source 3: CDC HIV Nexus\">[3]</a></p>\n<h2 id=\"make-room-for-emotions-without-replacing-the-evidence\">Make room for emotions without replacing the evidence</h2>\n<p>Someone may understand U=U intellectually and still feel anxious. Another person may be tired of having their health treated as a threat. Those feelings deserve an honest conversation, but they do not change the underlying prevention evidence.</p>\n<p>Try separating “What do I know?” from “What am I afraid of?” The first question may need accurate information from a clinician. The second may need time, reflection or qualified support. Avoid asking a partner to accept stigma as the price of reassuring you.</p>\n<h2 id=\"build-a-broader-sexual-health-plan\">Build a broader sexual-health plan</h2>\n<p>This original Vulpes Ignis discussion sheet has four parts: what we understand about U=U; any unanswered clinical questions; other STI and pregnancy-prevention choices; and how we would handle a change in care or exposure.</p>\n<p>Agree on a realistic way to communicate changes rather than a promise that nobody will ever face a practical difficulty. A prevention plan can be clear without becoming a courtroom.</p>\n<p>U=U helps people make decisions from better information. Treatment, dignity and enjoyable relationships belong in the same conversation.</p>",
    "practice": "If U=U is part of your care or relationship conversation, ask the HIV care team how viral-suppression monitoring fits your plan. Discuss other prevention questions separately.",
